Handle every field gSPModifyVertex can change

gSPModifyVertex (G_MODIFYVTX) changes one field of a vertex that G_VTX has already loaded and transformed: its colour
(G_MWO_POINT_RGBA), texture coordinates (G_MWO_POINT_ST), screen position (G_MWO_POINT_XYSCREEN) or screen depth
(G_MWO_POINT_ZSCREEN). Fast3D handled only ST and asserted on the others (SUPPORT_CHECK), so a debug build stopped at
the first other one and a release build wrote its value into the texture coordinates. WCW/nWo Revenge draws its
sprites this way: VTX, four ZSCREEN, four XYSCREEN, RGBA and ST, then a QUAD.

The number formats come from the N64 SDK. XYSCREEN holds x in its high half and y in its low half, each signed with
2 fraction bits like the viewport's scale and translation (gbi.h's Vp comment). ZSCREEN holds a screen depth with 16
fraction bits in the units of the viewport's z scale and translation: gbi.h gives screen depth 10 integer bits
(G_MAXZ) plus 16 fraction bits, and its G_DEPTOZS builds this number for gSPBranchLessZ, which compares it with the
same field of the vertex. With the usual viewport (G_MAXZ / 2 for z scale and translation) 0 is the near plane and
0x3FE << 16 the far one.

The N64 keeps screen coordinates for a loaded vertex; Fast3D keeps clip coordinates and lets the GPU divide by w and
apply the viewport. So CalcAndSetViewport now keeps a copy of the viewport (RSP::viewport), and a new screen position
or depth is worked back through it into clip coordinates at the vertex's own w (x then goes through
AdjXForAspectRatio, as in GfxSpVertex). Keeping w keeps 1/w, and with it perspective-correct texturing, as on the
N64. A depth beyond the near or far plane is moved onto that plane, because the GPU would clip it away. After a new
position or depth the trivial-rejection flags are worked out again (GfxSpVertex's code for them is now
ClipRejectFlags, used by both). The N64 keeps the flags G_VTX set, but those are for its clip box, usually larger
than the screen, while Fast3D's are for the screen itself; keeping them would throw away a sprite whose vertices were
loaded off screen. RGBA replaces the colour and the alpha, which holds the fog factor when G_FOG is on, as on the
N64. An unknown field is now logged and ignored instead of asserting, and so is a vertex number past the end of the
vertex buffer (the old code wrote past it).

Not in LibUltraShip main on 2026-11-08; drop this patch once it is there.

